{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the 12th century Crusader states particularly in the County of Tripoli. He ruled from 1152 to 1199 leaving a significant mark on the region's political and military landscape. His reign was marked by both successes and challenges facing the Crusader states during a period of increasing Muslim power and internal strife.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county at a young age in 1152 following his father's death. His early reign saw him consolidate his power and strengthen the county's defenses against various threats including Muslim raids and internal rebellions. He was known for his military prowess and his ability to maintain a strong army. He also fostered alliances with other Crusader rulers.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by a complex interplay of diplomacy warfare and internal politics. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous Muslim incursions often relying on his military strength and strategic alliances. He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Edessa. His policies aimed to maintain Tripoli’s independence and prosperity. He invested in infrastructure and promoted trade fostering economic growth within the county. He was a skilled administrator and attempted to improve the lives of his subjects.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III’s military campaigns were a defining feature of his reign. He led numerous expeditions to repel Muslim attacks particularly along the Mediterranean coast. He maintained a well-equipped army composed of knights infantry and mercenaries. His military successes helped to secure Tripoli’s borders and protect its trade routes. He also participated in conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states often vying for influence and territory. These conflicts were often costly and resulted in significant casualties. He faced challenges from both Muslim forces and internal factions within the Crusader states.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III’s reign concluded in 1199. He died while leading an expedition against the Muslim forces. His death was a significant loss for the County of Tripoli. He is remembered as a capable and courageous ruler who defended Tripoli’s independence and prosperity during a turbulent period. His legacy is one of military strength political acumen and effective governance. He is often regarded as one of the most successful Crusader rulers of the 12th century. His actions helped to shape the political and military landscape of the Levant for decades to come. He left behind a stable and relatively prosperous county despite the constant threats it faced.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1804991215467453}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.115249561844394e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08993984013795853}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.037541281431913376}, {"token": "▁prominent", "logprob": -1.274379014968872}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.021645264700055122}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.005288183689117432}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.02645823545753956}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.8220901489257812}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0019252828788012266}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.10461129993200302}, {"token": "▁Crus", "logprob": -1.2648131847381592}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.08130882680416107}, {"token": "▁states", "logprob": -0.11759599298238754}, {"token": "▁particularly", "logprob": -1.0068000555038452}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.7008093595504761}, {"to